  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nAn upper ridge extends from the southeast part of the country and \nthe southern Plains to the central Plains and parts of the Midwest. \nAn upper trough is centered over the Pacific Northwest. South \ncentral and central Nebraska and north central Kansas are under the \nnorthern edge of the upper ridge. A surface trough over western \nNebraska and western Kansas extends eastward across the forecast \narea. Winds across the area are mostly out of the south to \nsoutheast. High temperatures today are expected to range from the \nlow 90s to around 101 degrees. Heat index values are expected to \nrange from the mid 90s to near 109 degrees. High temperatures \nSaturday will be similar or slightly cooler than today with heat \nindex values in the mid 90s to around 105 degrees. A Heat Advisory \nis in effect for all of north central Kansas and portions of south \ncentral and central Nebraska and continues into Saturday evening. \n\nSome showers and isolated thunderstorms are moving across portions \nof south central and central Nebraska with an upper disturbance \noverhead/near the area. This convection and increased cloud cover \nmay keep temperatures today across portions of the area from \nreaching expected highs. These showers and isolated storms may \ncontinue through most, if not all, of the afternoon. Showers and \nstorms may redevelop across northwestern portions of the area \ntonight. Low temperatures tonight will range from the mid 60s to mid \n70s. Near record warm low temperatures are possible tonight for \nGrand Island and Hastings. The record warm lows are in the mid 70s \nwhile the expected low temperatures are in the low 70s. Similar low \ntemperatures are expected for tomorrow night. Additional storms may \nmove across northern and western portions of the area Saturday \nafternoon and evening with the arrival of another shortwave. Some of \nthese storms have the potential to become strong to marginally \nsevere given fairly high CAPE and lapse rates although wind shear \nwill be the limiting factor. \n\nThe upper ridge will shift around a bit Sunday and Monday but will \nmostly be over the southern and central Plains. High temperatures \nboth days may decrease slightly but are expected to mostly be in the \n90s. Heat index values both days are expected, at this time, to be \nbelow 105 degrees but will likely range from around 100 to 103 \ndegrees across (mainly southern) portions of the area. A cold front \nwill move through the area on Tuesday. This front will bring \nincreased (up to 55%) chances of showers and storms Monday night \nthrough Tuesday night. High temperatures on Tuesday may range from \nthe upper 80s to the low triple digits. The cold front will have \nmore impact on Wednesday with high temperatures mainly in the mid \n80s to mid 90s. Similar temperatures are expected for Thursday. Low \nrain and storm chances (15% to 25%) continue for portions of the \narea Wednesday and Thursday.",
